// Queen's Gambit Declined — the Watch course, one beat a move
// (WO-OPENINGS-REWRITE-01). Written from the fact sheet
// (`LESSON_FACTS=1 LESSON_IDS=qgd`): masters' percentages, engine lines at a
// fixed depth, the board after each move. The student plays Black. Standard:
// docs/OPENING-WATCH-BEATS.md, said in the think-out-loud register (§2a). One
// story runs through every line: …d5 and …e6 hold the center, the bishop on c8
// is the price, and the game turns on the freeing breaks (…c5, …e5) and on the
// road that bishop finds out.
import type { WatchCourse } from './watchCourse';

const MAIN = ['d4', 'd5', 'c4', 'e6', 'Nc3', 'Nf6', 'Bg5', 'Be7', 'e3', 'O-O', 'Nf3', 'Nbd7', 'Rc1', 'c6', 'Bd3', 'dxc4', 'Bxc4', 'Nd5', 'Bxe7', 'Qxe7', 'O-O', 'Nxc3', 'Rxc3', 'e5', 'Bb3', 'exd4', 'exd4', 'Nf6', 'Re1', 'Qd6', 'Ne5', 'Be6', 'Bxe6', 'fxe6', 'Qb3', 'Rab8'];

/** The main line's beats, in order — one per move of `MAIN`. */
const MAIN_BEATS: Array<[string, string]> = [
  ["d4 — the queen's pawn steps into the center, and the bishop on c1 can come out now. About a third of master games start here; e4 is more common, and the engine prefers it. Your answer in the Queen's Gambit Declined is the old, simple one: put a pawn of your own opposite this one, and never let go of that square.", 'd4 — center pawn, frees their dark bishop.'],
  ["…d5 — the classical reply, face to face with their pawn. Six masters in ten start with …Nf6 instead and about a quarter play this, and the engine rates them the same. Look at e4: your pawn already covers it, so if their e-pawn ever arrives there, you can simply take it.", '…d5 — meet the pawn head-on.'],
  ["c4 — the Queen's Gambit, the choice of about three masters in four. It's an offer: the c4 pawn has no guard, and your d5 pawn could take it. But think about what that pawn is doing first. It's your only foothold in the center, and c4 is trying to lure it away.", 'c4 — the gambit pawn challenges d5.'],
  ["…e6 — and this is the Queen's Gambit Declined. You don't take on c4. You put a second pawn behind d5 instead, so if they ever take there, your e-pawn takes back and a pawn stays in the middle. About a third of masters choose it, half play …c6, the Slav, and the engine rates …e6 as highly as anything. Now the honest part: look at your bishop on c8. The e6 pawn has just closed its diagonal, and that bishop is the price of all this solidity. Everything in the lines ahead turns on two questions. When do you break with …c5 or …e5? And how does that bishop get out?", '…e6 — the Declined: d5 held, c8 shut.'],
  ["Nc3 — about six masters in ten bring the knight out here, and now d5 is hit twice, by the c4 pawn and the knight. Count what holds it: your e6 pawn and the queen on d8. Two against two, so it stands, but your next move should add a guard.", 'Nc3 — a second hit on d5.'],
  ["…Nf6 — the natural developing move, played by about four masters in ten. The knight gives d5 a third guard and covers e4, so their e-pawn still can't step up for free. One thing to keep in mind: with your queen right behind it on d8, this knight is the piece they will often try to pin.", '…Nf6 — third guard on d5, covers e4.'],
  ["Bg5 — about a quarter of masters pin here, while just over half take on d5 first, the Exchange Variation. So what does the pin do? Your knight on f6 is one of the guards of d5, and now it can't move: if it does, Bxd8 takes your queen. The question for your next move is how to set that knight free.", 'Bg5 — the pin on f6.'],
  ["…Be7 — two masters in three answer this way, and it's the simplest fix: the bishop steps in between g5 and your queen, so the knight on f6 is no longer pinned. It also clears f8, so you can castle next. Why not d6 or b4? On d6 a pawn push to c5 would hit it, and on b4 a3 would.", '…Be7 — blocks the pin, castling next.'],
  ["e3 — three masters in four play it. The pawn shores up d4 and opens the road for their bishop on f1, so c4 is covered now: take there, and the bishop simply takes back. Remember that. You'd much rather take on c4 once that bishop has already spent its move somewhere else.", 'e3 — guards d4, covers c4.'],
  ["…O-O — over half of masters castle at once, and three in ten ask the bishop a question with …h6 first, the road to the Tartakower and the Lasker. Your king is tucked away on g8, and the setup is nearly complete: d5 held, king safe. What's left is the bishop on c8.", '…O-O — king safe; c8 still waits.'],
  ["Nf3 — three masters in four bring the knight to its best square. From f3 it covers e5, the square your e-pawn would like to reach one day. Keep that in mind: …e5 is one of your two freeing breaks, and this knight now stands guard over it next to their d4 pawn.", 'Nf3 — the knight watches e5.'],
  ["…Nbd7 — the Orthodox knight, and the engine's first choice; half of masters play …h6 here instead. Why d7 and not c6? On c6 the knight would stand in front of your c-pawn, and you need that pawn free for …c6 or …c5. From d7 it backs up the knight on f6 and covers e5, the square your other break needs.", '…Nbd7 — backs f6, keeps the c-pawn free.'],
  ["Rc1 — half the masters bring the rook to the c-file before the file is even open. Why so early? If the c-pawns ever come off, the rook is already standing on the file, pointed at your c-pawn. About a quarter prefer the quieter Qc2.", 'Rc1 — the rook waits on the c-file.'],
  ["…c6 — about half of masters play it. It looks modest, so what's the point? It gives d5 one more guard and takes b5 away from their knight. And it fits the plan you're heading for: take on c4 at the right moment, then free your game with a knight jump to d5, where this pawn will back it up. The engine slightly prefers …h6; after …c6 they are a little better.", '…c6 — props d5, covers b5.'],
  ["Bd3 — three masters in four develop the bishop here, aimed at your h7 pawn and ready to castle. But look at the timing. The bishop has just made its move, and their c4 pawn is still standing in front of your d5 pawn. If you take now, that bishop has to move a second time to take back. That's the moment you've been waiting for since e3.", 'Bd3 — aims at h7; now …dxc4 gains time.'],
  ["…dxc4 — seven masters in ten take now, and there's the timing: the pawn hits the bishop on d3, so it has to spend a second move taking back on c4. Yes, you give up your pawn on d5. In return the center opens, and your pieces finally get squares, starting with d5 for your knight.", '…dxc4 — trade with tempo on d3.'],
  ["Bxc4 — every master takes back, and material is level. The bishop on c4 now looks down at your e6 pawn. Take stock: you have every minor piece out except the one on c8. Now comes the move this whole setup was built around.", 'Bxc4 — material level, aimed at e6.'],
  ["…Nd5 — over eight masters in ten play it, and it's the classic freeing idea in this line. Look at what the knight does: it hits their knight on c3 and the e3 pawn, it gives your bishop on e7 a second guard, and it offers trades. Why trade? Your position is a little cramped, and every pair of pieces that comes off gives the rest of your army more room. With fewer pieces around, your …e5 break gets closer too.", '…Nd5 — hits c3, offers the trades.'],
  ["Bxe7 — almost every master trades the dark-squared bishops here. The bishop on e7 hits your queen and your rook on f8, and nothing guards it, so there's only one question: which piece takes back?", 'Bxe7 — trades bishops, hits d8 and f8.'],
  ["…Qxe7 — every master takes with the queen. What about …Nxc3 first, hitting their queen? It fails: Bxd8 takes yours, and you lose far more than you win. And …Nxe7 would pull your best knight back off d5. So the queen recaptures, and one pair of bishops is gone.", '…Qxe7 — not …Nxc3, which drops the queen.'],
  ["Castle. Six masters in ten tuck the king away, while about a third play Ne4, keeping their knight out of your trade offer. Both kings are safe now, and your knight on d5 is still hitting the one on c3.", 'O-O — their king tucked away on g1.'],
  ["…Nxc3 — nearly nine masters in ten make the trade. The knight lands on c3 hitting their queen on d1, so they have to take back, and a second pair of pieces leaves the board. Now count what's left in your way: their pawn on d4, and the bishop on c8 that still needs a road out.", '…Nxc3 — hits d1; a second trade.'],
  ["Rxc3 — nineteen masters in twenty take back with the rook; bxc3 would leave their a-pawn on its own. Two pairs of pieces are off, your position has room to breathe, and now comes the break this whole setup was built for.", 'Rxc3 — recaptures, pawns kept whole.'],
  ["…e5 — the freeing break, played by nearly nine masters in ten and the engine's choice. The pawn hits d4. Better still, it steps off e6, so once your knight on d7 moves, the bishop on c8 finally has a diagonal. The cost: with e6 empty, their bishop on c4 now pins your f7 pawn to your king.", "…e5 — hits d4, opens the c8 bishop's road."],
  ["Bb3 — masters split three ways here: Qc2 in three games of ten, and the trade on e5 and this retreat in about a quarter each. The trade on e5 is the Orthodox main line. The bishop keeps its aim at your f7 pawn, still pinned to your king, and leaves the tension on d4 for you to resolve.", 'Bb3 — keeps the pin on f7.'],
  ["…exd4 — just under half the masters take, and four in ten push past with …e4 instead. Taking makes them decide: the pawn on d4 hits the rook on c3 and the e3 pawn, so they have to recapture. Whichever way they do, the center opens up and your pieces get lines to work on.", '…exd4 — hits c3 and e3.'],
  ["exd4 — almost every master takes back with the e-pawn, and the e-file opens. Look at what's left: their d4 pawn has no pawn beside it on the c- or e-file. That's an isolated pawn, and from here on it's a target. The square in front of it, d5, is a post no pawn of theirs can ever chase you from.", 'exd4 — an isolated pawn on d4.'],
  ["…Nf6 — the knight comes back to its home square, the engine's first choice, and it does two jobs. It covers d5, the square in front of their isolated pawn. And it gets out of the way of your bishop on c8, whose diagonal is finally open.", '…Nf6 — watches d5, frees the c8 bishop.'],
  ["Re1 — the rook takes the open e-file and hits your queen on e7, which has no guard. So the queen has to move again. Where does it go?", 'Re1 — the e-file, with a hit on e7.'],
  ["…Qd6 — the queen steps off the e-file onto a square where it works. From d6 it hits their isolated pawn on d4 and looks down the diagonal at h2. That's the method against an isolated pawn: gather your pieces in front of it and around it, all leaning on the same target.", '…Qd6 — hits d4, aims at h2.'],
  ["Ne5 — the knight lands on e5, and no pawn of yours can kick it: your d-pawn is gone, and the f-pawn is still pinned by the bishop on b3. From e5 it hits your pawns on c6 and f7. That bishop on b3 is behind all of it, so it's the piece to go after.", 'Ne5 — hits c6 and f7; f7 is pinned.'],
  ["…Be6 — at last the bishop on c8 comes out, and it goes straight at the piece that has been pinning you: it hits the bishop on b3 and offers to trade it off. The engine prefers …Nd5, planting the knight in front of the isolated pawn first; after …Be6 they are a little better.", '…Be6 — challenges b3; …Nd5 was better.'],
  ["Bxe6 — they take, and their bishop on e6 has no guard, so you take back. For the moment they are clearly better on the board, and the only question is which piece recaptures.", 'Bxe6 — the trade; how to recapture?'],
  ["…fxe6 — the f-pawn takes back, and the engine agrees. Why not the queen? After …Qxe6, Ng6 hits your rook on f8 and opens their rook on e1 onto your queen at the same time, and you end up giving the queen for a rook and a knight. With the pawn, your rook on f8 gets a half-open file, and the new e6 pawn covers d5, the square in front of their isolated pawn. The price is a lone pawn of your own on e6.", '…fxe6 — the f-file opens for your rook.'],
  ["Qb3 — the queen hits two of your pawns at once: b7, which has no guard, and e6, which is pinned to your king on g8 along the diagonal. So the b-pawn needs help first.", 'Qb3 — hits b7 and the pinned e6.'],
  ["…Rab8 — the rook guards b7, and they are a little better for now. Your plan from here: plant a knight on d5, right in front of their isolated pawn, where no pawn of theirs can ever chase it. Once the knight leaves f6, your rook on f8 looks straight down the half-open f-file at f2, and your queen keeps leaning on d4.", '…Rab8 — guards b7; …Nd5 comes next.'],
];

const beats: Record<string, { say: string; sayShort: string }> = {};
MAIN_BEATS.forEach(([say, sayShort], k) => { beats[MAIN.slice(0, k + 1).join(' ')] = { say, sayShort }; });

